g is set, calling `strlen(NULL)` would segfault. So this code assumes if you have only a shortArg, you must not be the matching path. But the for loop condition is `options[0].longArg || options[0].shortArg`. So entering the loop with longArg=NULL but shortArg=set is fine (size==1 case). But the else-if calls `strlen(options[0].longArg)` which is segfault. Hmm, in practice if the short arg matches, we break before reaching else-if. If short arg doesn't match, we segfault.\n\nActually wait, re-checking - if `size == 1 && options[0].shortArg == arg[0]` is false because shortArg is '\\0', then we still go to else-if. So if longArg is NULL, it'll segfault. This is a C bug.\n\nThe Rust version handles this safely by using `if let Some(la) = &o.long_arg`. Different behavior, but it's a Rust safety improvement.\n\nNow let me look at corner cases. Let me also think about which parts of `xopt_default_callback` are reachable in Rust without unsafe pointer manipulation - the Rust version doesn't actually write to the data pointer, just validates. So the test for parsing logic is mostly via callbacks or via the success/failure of parse.\n\nLet me design my tests:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":340,"cache_read_input_tokens":88305,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":340,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ad17ac0-0fb9-4195-a719-c8bd0d489905","uuid":"eb19e0de-6baf-4e9c-969f-60cd953ed421"}
